body:
Agentic authoring (library form): `kalide init-library <path>` writes an `AGENTS.md` at the library root — a single Markdown file giving coding agents a complete, accurate reference for authoring a kalide template library. The guide is written ONLY when no `AGENTS.md` already exists at the library root: a pre-existing `AGENTS.md` never blocks init-library and is left byte-for-byte untouched (the library is still created normally). It is the library/template-author counterpart of the deck guide (agent-authoring): it documents the same format but leads with library authoring so an agent can create and extend templates. It covers: the `templates/` layout (`library.yaml`, `slides/<name>/`, `sections/<name>/`, `themes/<name>/`, `media/` — templates-dir-layout, library-guide-layout); `library.yaml` (name identifier `[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*`, optional `description`, required `format: 1`); the `template.yaml` schema and the `example.md` convention (template-manifest, field-types, body-rules, template-composition, template-variants, number-date-formats); the Go html/template layout language with the helper set (`media`, `section`, `dict`, `list` — template-language, section-helper, dict-list-helpers) and the reserved `.deck`/`.slide`/`.item`/`.raw`/`.data` context (template-context, item-context, raw-source-context, section-data-context, deck-data-in-templates), plus the reserved names `deck`/`slide`/`item`/`raw`/`data` (requirements.constraint.reserved-template-names); themes (token-only) and media reference rules — the layout `{{ media }}` helper, theme-owned files referenced from CSS served out of a theme directory by relative `url()`, and the reserved `media:` (the library's shared `media/` tree) and `theme:<name>/` (another theme's files) URL prefixes, both valid for `url()` and `@import` and both available in any CSS the theme imports, so one theme can build on another (theme-selection, template-media, theme-media-references); and how to validate the library by pointing a deck at it and running `kalide start`, or listing it with `kalide templates`. It states that a library with no templates is load-valid but a library intended to host a deck provides the deck's theme (external-template-library, theme-selection). Like the deck guide, it is written verbatim from a static file embedded in internal/scaffold, is text-only and offline, is not a template asset, and stays outside the load/validate/render/list/serve paths (global.constraint.no-embedded-template-assets, requirements.constraint.brand-not-embedded). The library layout ignores non-library root entries, so this guide is permitted (library-guide-layout).

AC:
- immediately after `kalide init-library ../lib`, `../lib/AGENTS.md` exists and `../lib` still loads cleanly (templates-dir-validation);
- a deck pointed at `../lib` still validates and serves (the guide is inert);
- the guide documents every field type, the `media`/`section`/`dict`/`list` helpers, the reserved context keys (`.deck`, `.slide`, `.item`, `.raw`, `.data`) and the `template.yaml` keys, so an agent can author a new template without reading Go source;
- the guide documents the reserved `media:` and `theme:<name>/` theme-CSS prefixes (theme-media-references), so an author can share one `media/` asset set across themes and layer one theme on another;
- the guide drops the rules that a theme reaches only its own directory and that `media:` is forbidden on `@import`, and documents the library-wide reach;
- scaffolding into a library directory that already holds an `AGENTS.md` succeeds and leaves that file byte-for-byte unchanged; the scaffolded `AGENTS.md` is written only when none exists.
